      "card_ids": ["C10"],
      "claim": "A guanidine-containing compound (NY0128) crossed the BBB and accumulated in rat brain, but its structure is dominated by a bulky triphenylmethyl ether that provides the lipophilicity absent in the query; therefore it does not establish meaningful access for the dicationic query.",
      "evidence_role": "contradictory"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C05"],
      "claim": "The highest-similarity analogue is a zwitterionic amino acid piperidine reported to cross the BBB in humans, but it lacks the dicationic guanidine-azocane core, so its transfer to the query is limited.",
      "evidence_role": "contradictory"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C08"],
      "claim": "Lipophilic basic amines such as pitolisant cross the BBB, but they contain substantial aromatic/lipophilic groups and are not dicationic; they do not outweigh the query's complete ionization and very low logD.",
      "evidence_role": "contradictory"
    }
  ],
  "confidence": "high",
  "decision_summary": "The query is guanethidine, a dicationic guanidine-azocane with zero neutral fraction and very low logD at pH 7.4. Its known pharmacology is peripheral adrenergic blockade with poor CNS penetration. The only polar ionized amine analogue with direct brain-uptake data (C09) shows low uptake, while the many BBB-penetrant basic amines are lipophilic and lack the dicationic guanidine. Therefore the molecule is predicted to lack meaningful CNS access.",
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "No direct BBB/PK measurement for guanethidine itself in the provided evidence set.",
    "All evidence analogues have low Morgan similarity to the query.",
    "No efflux transporter or active-uptake assay data are available for the query.",
    "No human CSF or brain concentration data are available for the query."
  ],
